# Day 4 — Encoded or Obfuscated PowerShell Execution

Day 4 continues the PowerShell focus from Day 3 by looking for commands that may be attempting to conceal their true purpose.

This remains a single-table query using `DeviceProcessEvents`, but introduces regular-expression extraction and simple categorisation of different obfuscation methods.

---

## Objective

Identify PowerShell processes using encoded commands or command-line elements commonly associated with obfuscation.

Examples may include:

```powershell
powershell.exe -EncodedCommand SQBFAFgA...
```

```powershell
pwsh.exe -ec SQBFAFgA...
```

```powershell
powershell.exe -Command IEX(...)
```

```powershell
powershell.exe -Command [Convert]::FromBase64String(...)
```

PowerShell encoding and obfuscation can be used to conceal malicious commands from users, analysts and basic security controls.

However, encoded PowerShell is not automatically malicious and may also be used by legitimate administration, automation or security tools.

---

